# Insulin Delivery

The Insulin Delivery report answers the question "how much insulin do I use,
and how is it split?". It shows your total daily dose and the split between
basal and bolus insulin.

<Video id="insulin-delivery" />

## What it shows

Your total daily dose (TDD) has two parts:

- **Basal insulin**: the background insulin that covers the needs of your body
  between meals.
- **Bolus insulin**: the insulin for meals and to correct high glucose.

The page says that a typical split is about 50/50. The split changes with diet,
activity and your own needs.

### The key figures

| Figure | What it shows |
|--------|---------------|
| **Avg TDD** | Your average total insulin each day |
| **Basal** | The basal part of your insulin, as a percentage, with the total units |
| **Bolus** | The bolus part of your insulin, as a percentage, with the total units |
| **Boluses/Day** | The number of boluses each day, with the average bolus size |
| **ICR** | Your insulin-to-carb ratio: the grams of your meals for each unit of their boluses. The note shows the carbs that had no bolus, such as hypo treatments. The ratio does not include them |

### Daily Basal/Bolus Breakdown

Each bar is one day. The lower part of the bar is basal insulin. The upper part
is bolus insulin. Move the pointer over a bar to see the units for that day.

### Hourly Insulin Delivery

The chart shows the average insulin at each hour of the day, split into basal
and bolus. If your data has temp basals, the basal part is split into
**Scheduled Basal** and **Temp Basal**.

Under the chart, the units are added up for three parts of the day: **Morning
(6am-12pm)**, **Afternoon (12pm-6pm)** and **Evening/Night**. Evening/Night is
6pm to 6am, so it covers 12 hours. The other two parts cover 6 hours each.

### Bolus Breakdown

This card shows the **Total Boluses**, and how many were **Meal Boluses** and
**Correction Boluses**.

### Clinical Reference

This card compares your TDD with a typical range for type 1 diabetes: 0.4 to 1.0
units for each kilogram of body weight. It also says what your insulin-to-carb
ratio means: 1 unit of insulin for each number of grams of carbs.

## How to use it

The report opens on the last 30 days. To see if your last week is like the rest
of the month:

1. Look at the **Avg TDD**, **Basal** and **Bolus** figures for 30 days.
2. At the top of the report, select the date range.
3. Select **7 Days**.
4. Select **Apply Filters**. The report loads again for 7 days.
5. Compare the figures with the figures for 30 days.

To see how your basal rate changes through the day, select **Basal Analysis**
to open the [Basal Rate Analysis](/docs/reports/basal-analysis).

## Good to know

- If the period has too few days with data, a badge under the date shows how
  many days the report uses.
- If the period has no basal or bolus records, the daily chart shows **No
  insulin data available**.

<Callout type="warning" title="Talk to your care team first">

  The total daily dose, the basal/bolus split and the ICR help your care
  team review your settings. Do not change your doses or pump settings because
  of these figures alone. Discuss changes with your diabetes care team.

</Callout>
